WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.563
- I do believe that we
need to reframe success.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.940
We have to, for the fucking sake

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.070
of so many fucking millions of people,

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:10.630
we have to make success happiness.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.080
We have to, have to, have to, have to.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.930
- Hey everybody, this is David Neagle

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.760
and welcome to the
Successful Mind Podcast.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.390
Today, we are in New York at VaynerMedia

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.390
and we have our special
guest Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.160
Gary, thank you so much for doing this.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.120
- Really excited to be here.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.760
- I'm very excited to
talk to you about this

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.950
because you talk about
something as self awareness.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.232
You say that self awareness is the key.

00:00:36.232 --> 00:00:39.110
And my number one question for you

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.800
is how did you become self aware?

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:41.633
When did that happen for you?

00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:43.570
When did it all click in?

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:44.911
You're like this is who I am,

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:45.744
this is what I'm here to do

00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:49.210
and then you were like nose
to the grindstone with it?

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.511
- In some way when I hear that question

00:00:51.511 --> 00:00:53.350
what feels natural to me

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.510
is an evolution between 11 and 18.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.810
Somewhere between definitely fourth grade

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:05.200
when I had the conviction and the guts

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.980
to have weird little
conversations with myself in bed

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.413
when I would be anxious

00:01:09.413 --> 00:01:12.267
about the fact that I was
making this transition

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:14.360
to being a bad student.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:15.675
I don't know how else to say it.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.840
In third grade, I got straight A's

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.600
and would win in flash cards

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.150
'cause I have good math skills

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.124
and in fourth grade, I literally
started getting bad grades

00:01:24.124 --> 00:01:27.713
and A, it didn't come
natural to me anymore.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.630
Now I think my mind
was wandering in class.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.760
So the ability to just
listen and go off of that

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.650
to be successful became a problem

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:39.990
because now I was
already starting to think

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.340
about how to sell baseball
cards to my friends,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.000
what am I gonna do after school,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.060
let me make a joke to
make everybody laugh,

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.750
my brain started to wander

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.960
and then it was also around the time

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:50.970
when it was time to actually

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.170
do stuff at home, like homework,

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.470
and that was unacceptable

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.550
'cause I had to play Nerf football

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.120
and try to sell and video games.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.620
So between that moment

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.130
and even right up to
senior year of high school

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.220
navigating that and understanding

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.350
this is what I'm good at,

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.802
this is what I'm not good at,

00:02:09.802 --> 00:02:12.130
and then really in high school

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.190
intuitively, subconsciously, naturally,

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.910
being able to not have to fit in

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.120
and not being affected by,

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.880
look, it's really interesting,

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.480
I think anybody who's listening,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.300
maybe this is Jersey thing

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.490
but everybody made fun of
everybody all the time always.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.668
That was just the nature of, right?

00:02:31.668 --> 00:02:34.730
I think a lot about what
are we doing to children

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:35.930
in our parenting now

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:37.866
that make it so difficult?

00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:39.910
And extreme bullying is very different

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.220
than getting made fun of occasionally

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.600
and so there's a lot of variables to this

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.260
but the high school years of like

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.800
not worrying about my
popularity and valuing,

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.490
one of the things that I'm most proud of

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:55.930
is getting DM's and messages
from high school friends today

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.360
saying, man, this is so cool

00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.840
'cause this was always actually you.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.320
You literally have the, verbatim,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:03.153
you had the guts

00:03:03.153 --> 00:03:05.110
to be friends with
everybody in high school.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.100
And I was very proud of that.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.100
I was very proud of
weirdest kid on the bus.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:10.860
- So you didn't try to fit in?

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.930
- I thought fitting in was
fitting in with everybody

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.220
instead of fitting in to like six people.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.980
So I wanted to fit in with the kid

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.390
that nobody was nice to,

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.790
I wanted to fit in to the kids

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.710
that were naturally
kind of my core friends

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.170
who were like into sports or sports cards.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.397
I wanted to fit in with popular kids,

00:03:27.397 --> 00:03:29.730
and that was easy too,

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.400
I am who I am,

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.950
obviously, I have people skills

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.750
but I was aware that it was unusual

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.630
how I was navigating through high school,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.630
not going to parties, but working,

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.403
being friends with the weird kids,

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.170
not overpandering to the
coolest kids in our school,

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.673
not being good at grades.

00:03:49.821 --> 00:03:51.380
I was an enigma my whole life

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.350
and I think 11 to 18

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:55.470
was when that manifested

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.030
and that's a long-winded answer to

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.910
that's when I started understanding myself

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.350
which is what I would
argue self awareness was.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:04.570
It was also when I went through

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.460
wait a minute, I'm not
the cutest kid in class,

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.410
wait a minute, I'm not the best athlete

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.034
because in second and fourth, fifth grade,

00:04:11.034 --> 00:04:13.170
in fourth grade, I thought
I was the best looking,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.670
most athletic kid in my class

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:19.270
and very honestly I think
there's a chance I was but, but--

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:21.010
- But that's because also because your mom

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:21.880
was pumping you up,

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.867
like she was like positive all the way.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.500
- Yes, that and I was competitive

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.350
and I have great hand eye coordination

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:30.670
and I matured slowly

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.190
so I had boyish looks.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.960
Yes, all those things but it was a great,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.830
I was not scared of all the bad stuff

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:40.170
and I think we live in a world right now

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.953
where people are scared of the bad stuff.

00:04:41.953 --> 00:04:42.786
- [David] Tremendously.

00:04:42.786 --> 00:04:44.850
- And I believe that that's
what leads to delusion.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.030
You don't want to face that
you're not the fastest,

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:49.410
you're not the best looking,

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.870
you're not the smartest.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:52.370
You don't want to face that.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.040
You don't want to accept that

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:57.370
which leads to a lack of self awareness.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:00.040
- So I'm kind of guessing

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.490
that you were sensitive kid.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.840
I hear you cried a lot,
you got your feelings hurt?

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:06.980
- By the way, I'm gonna
give you a huge dap,

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.530
that is the first time
that somebody has said that

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.670
and it couldn't be more true.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:18.670
I cried constantly from 1982 to 1988

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:25.633
and I mean multiple times
a week, real crying.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:27.920
- So that brings me to another question.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.580
So you talked about when you
went to work with your dad

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.940
and he kind of sensed your bullshit right,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.380
like you could really bullshit people

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:39.550
and you made a comment
on one of your podcasts

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.700
that there were many times
you were in his office crying.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:43.533
- Yes.

00:05:43.533 --> 00:05:45.273
- What was he doing?

00:05:45.273 --> 00:05:46.500
- You know what's funny,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.930
him saying to me like,

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.373
the number's the number,

00:05:51.557 --> 00:05:53.340
my dad doesn't talk a lot,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.350
so he was a man from the old school.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.470
So it wasn't like he sat me down and said,

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:01.220
son, this embellishing
or lying is unacceptable,

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:02.410
it wasn't like that,

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.470
it would be like a customer would walk in

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.730
and I would trey to sell
them a bottle of wine,

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:13.430
and you want to talk about
not being self aware,

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:18.430
the audacity that I had as a
14-year-old who looked nine

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.530
to have a grownup come
into a liquor store,

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:22.940
hand them the bottle of wine

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:23.940
that I knew we had to sell

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.310
and be like you should buy this,

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.520
and they would look at me and say,

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.870
well, have you had it?

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:33.560
And I would say yes with
a straight fucking face

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.210
and really like believed it

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.330
is pretty ludicrous

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:41.230
and what would happen is
he would give me a look

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:44.010
and I could sense like oh I
did something wrong, not right

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:45.690
even though I accomplished the goal.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.820
And very quickly it started chipping away.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.550
The reason I would cry

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.940
is my dad is tough.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.270
I would be a 15-year-old kid,

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.200
I would work 11 hours,

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.250
like we'd leave the house at seven a.m.,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.120
the store, got there at 8:10,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.820
my dad would do the deposit,

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:06.860
I'd have to keep myself busy,

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:07.940
the store opened at nine,

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.720
it's now seven p.m.,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.070
no lunch break, I've worked very minute,

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.880
this is like child fucking
hardcore real labor

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.840
and then at 6:50 I would go to my dad

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:20.510
and muster up the guts to be like,

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.720
hey dad, are we going home soon?

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.233
And he would like bark at me.

00:07:29.369 --> 00:07:31.080
And that made me cry, I don't know,

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.080
it was like sad, I was like
fucking like fuck, this is hard.

00:07:37.152 --> 00:07:38.910
And the truth is actually

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.110
I'm embellishing a little bit there,

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.900
not that that, that would upset me,

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.250
what made me cry actually

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:49.023
is my dad genuinely did
not treat employees well,

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.580
and I'm sensitive to it as
I'm saying it right now.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:57.580
He transformed in the way that
he changed my ability to lie

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.193
I changed his point of view on employees.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.860
He just wasn't nice.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.420
I'd watch people really work.

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:07.400
He came from the Soviet Union.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:11.210
He looked at employees like not the way,

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:12.760
genuinely the complete opposite

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:15.020
of the way I look at employees.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.760
I look at them

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.240
and I just know what I feel

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.180
and I feel a sense of responsibility

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.840
and my dad genuinely
felt like I'm paying you

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.563
and fucking work, like
fuck you, like really

00:08:27.563 --> 00:08:29.693
and that really bothered me.

00:08:31.053 --> 00:08:33.290
- Yeah, well the guy was
working for his fucking life.

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:35.529
What a break to get
out of the Soviet Union

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:36.600
and be able to come
here and do what he did.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.163
- I judged him immensely at the time.

00:08:40.618 --> 00:08:43.830
Look, there's people that
have come from hard places

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:45.610
that don't treat their
employees like shit.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.230
So I still judge it at some level.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:51.163
I understand it a lot more.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:54.580
He really feared, really feared,

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.090
was crippled by the notion

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:58.680
of people stealing from him, crippled.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.390
- Did that happen a lot
in the Soviet Union?

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.284
Was that was the deal?

00:09:02.284 --> 00:09:03.117
- Oh, so I don't know if you know this.

00:09:03.117 --> 00:09:04.010
This is one of the most fascinating things

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.300
to talk to Americans about.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:08.010
I don't think Americans
actually really genuinely

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.400
understand socialism or communism.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:11.233
- Agree.

00:09:11.233 --> 00:09:12.931
- 'Cause they can't, I don't blame.

00:09:12.931 --> 00:09:14.450
- Everything was stolen.

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:15.283
- [David] Everything was stolen.

00:09:15.283 --> 00:09:16.390
- The government owns everything.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:18.570
So imagine every store and every company

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.550
is owned by the government.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.400
So I Gary, don't own Vayner,

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.300
I'm some general manager of,

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:30.250
not compensated in any
way based on its success.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:32.360
So now I'm making a deal with Dustin

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:33.550
that he can come in at night

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:36.180
and steal the computers and
sell 'em on the black market

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:37.560
but kick me some cash,

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:38.660
everything was stolen.

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.663
The entire country ran
on the black market.

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:45.450
Think about what's illegal in America,

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.602
drugs, black market,

00:09:47.602 --> 00:09:49.700
guns at some level, a certain type,

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.900
but everything that's illegal
here is on the black market.

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:54.690
- And everything is over there.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.690
- Everything, milk and
tile and carpet and chairs.

00:10:00.709 --> 00:10:02.820
People waited 13 years,

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.600
my parents waited 13 years to get a phone

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.460
and 20 years to get the
line for a refrigerator

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:11.790
and 15 for a car

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.873
and you waited seven
hours in line for bread.

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:20.730
When I talk about utter ridiculousness

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:24.090
of entitlement in America
in its current state,

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.970
seven dollar coffees,

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:29.320
complaining about Delta
being late by 10 minutes,

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:32.490
I not only come from a kid
that grew up in America

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.730
in a very immigrant household

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.940
that never bought anything
for anybody about anything,

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.810
I also come as the byproduct of parents

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:41.290
and stories my entire childhood

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:42.830
of ludicrous shit

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:45.567
that not a person here can understand,

00:10:45.567 --> 00:10:46.900
not here in this room,

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:48.530
not anybody who's listening.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:51.720
Go wait seven hours in
line for a piece of bread

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.290
and have access to no other food.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.400
Go, show me, show me how
much you're gonna cry

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:59.833
about not getting as many
likes on Instagram posts.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:03.290
- There's this huge
breeding of entitlement

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:05.480
that is going on in the country that is--

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:06.560
- It's called prosperity.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.070
It's why empires fall,

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.030
Rome did it, Genghis Kahn
did it, we're doing it.

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:12.280
We are gonna lose.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:13.610
China already won.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:16.812
This is just watching it play out.

00:11:16.812 --> 00:11:18.130
This is over.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:19.620
Every little Chinese kid

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.993
is way more hungrier
than our little fuckers.

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:26.320
And guess what, I say good.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:28.060
I don't want it to be, this is my team,

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:29.365
this is the jersey I'm wearing--

00:11:29.365 --> 00:11:30.420
- Do you think we rebound from it?

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:33.000
- No, Europe hasn't rebounded from it.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.450
We beat the fuck out of them.

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:36.643
No, there's no rebounding

